EXPERIMENT 01
Title: Practice Basic Bash, I/O Redirection, Pipes, File and Directory Operations, Searching Files, Process Management and Text Editors.
Aim
To practice basic Bash commands, I/O redirection, pipes, file and directory operations, searching files, process management and text editors.
Theory
1. pwd: Displays the current absolute path of the working directory.

2. cd: Changes the current directory.

3. cd ..: Moves up one directory level towards the root directory.

4. mkdir MIET: Creates a new directory named MIET.

5. mkdir CSE AIML CYBER: Creates multiple directories in the current path.

6. rmdir MIET: Removes an empty directory named MIET.

7. rm -r MIET: Recursively deletes a directory along with all of its nested contents.

8. ls: Lists visible files and folders in the current directory.

9. ls -l: Displays detailed file information.

10. ls -a: Lists all files, including hidden files.

11. touch file1.txt: Creates an empty file named file1.txt.

12. echo "word": Prints the text in terminal output.

13. cp file1.txt file2.txt: Copies the source file content into a new destination file.

14. mv file.txt file2.txt: Renames a file or moves it to another location.

15. cat file1.txt: Displays the entire content of a file.

16. head -n 1 file2.txt: Displays the first specified number of lines of a file.

17. sort file.txt: Reads a file and prints its contents in alphabetical order.

18. cat > file1.txt: Overwrites a file with new content.

19. cat >> file1.txt: Appends new lines to a file.

20. ls > file.txt: Redirects the standard output into a file.

21. ls | grep "file": Sends the output of ls as input to grep using a pipe to filter specific text.

22. tree: Displays the folder structure as a visual tree-like diagram.
Process Management Theory
1. jobs: Checks the running and stopped jobs in the current shell.

2. sleep 500: Makes the terminal wait for 500 seconds.

3. sleep 500 &: Runs the sleep command in the background.

4. kill %4: Terminates job number 4.

5. bg %5: Resumes a stopped job in the background.

6. fg %1: Brings a background or stopped job to the foreground.

7. ps: Displays information and status of running processes.

8. ps -e: Displays all running processes.

9. ps -ef: Displays detailed information about all processes, including their parent process IDs.

10. ps aux: Displays detailed information about processes, including memory and CPU usage.

11. kill -9 PID: Immediately terminates a process.

12. kill -15 PID: Sends a termination signal to a process.

13. kill -STOP PID: Stops a running process.

14. ps -o pid,ni,cmd: Displays the process ID, nice value (priority) and command name.

15. renice -4 -p PID: Changes the priority of a running process.

16. top: Displays real-time process information and system resource usage.
Conclusion
Successfully practised basic Linux Bash commands, file and directory operations, input/output redirection, pipes, searching files and process management. Understood how to create, copy, move and delete files and directories, and how to monitor and control processes using Linux commands.
